            "A full scan reads every page of the table once, in order, so its cost is proportional to N with a small per-row constant. An index lookup pays a fixed B-tree descent of h pages and then pays for the rows it returns; because those rows arrive in index order they are usually scattered across the heap, so each one costs a random page fetch with r roughly an order of magnitude larger than a sequential one. Adding an index becomes a bad idea exactly when s N r exceeds N r_seq, that is when s exceeds r_seq/r. That threshold is why a predicate matching half the table should not use an index while one matching two percent should.",
