            "EAT is a weighted average of the two service times, weighted by how often each occurs, so a rare event with a very large cost dominates as soon as it is no longer rare. Solving the same equation for p gives the budget: how many faults per access the design can absorb before performance leaves its target. The numbers are unforgiving because t_fault is roughly five orders of magnitude larger than t_hit. A five-percent fault rate is not a five-percent slowdown; it is a machine that spends most of its time waiting for a device.",
