            "The processor first pays the L1 hit time on every access. On the fraction m_L1 of accesses that miss, it pays the L2 hit time, and on the fraction m_L2 of those it continues to memory. Explanding the recursion one level happens by multiplying the conditional probabilities, which is why the two levels enter as a product rather than a sum of miss rates. The practical consequence is that a level-two miss rate is conditioned on level-one misses: 2 % L1 misses with a 25 % local L2 miss rate means only 0.5 % of all accesses reach memory. Effective design work therefore attacks the level where the product of frequency and cost is largest.",
